    "_summary": "Examples of procedural and random generation.",
    "_description": "# Procedural Generation Samples\r\n\r\nPlayMaker 2 includes a growing set of **runtime procedural generation examples**, each demonstrating a classic algorithm that can be combined, customized, or extended with FSM logic.\r\n\r\n{height=300}![screenshot](Packages/com.hutonggames.playmaker/Editor/Addons/Packages/com.hutonggames.playmaker.samples.proc-gen.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Maps\n\nThese samples show how various [**Procedural actions**](category:Actions/Procedural) can be combined with \nsimple **tilemap actions** like [**TilemapFillRect**](action:TilemapFillRect) and [**TilemapSetCells**](action:TilemapSetCells), to build entire levels.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Overview\r\n\r\n| Sample | Algorithm | Description |\r\n|---------|------------|--------------|\r\n| ****Rogue Map**** | Grid-based Rooms + Corridors | Classic **Rogue-like dungeon generator.** <br>Places rectangular rooms on a grid and connects them with corridors. <br>Great for traditional dungeon layouts. |\r\n| ****Cave Map**** | Diffusion-Limited Aggregation (DLA) | **Particles grow and stick** to form vein-like clusters. <br>Produces organic, branching cave systems. <br>Perfect for alien biomes or mineral formations. |\r\n| ****Tunnel Map**** | Drunkard’s Walk | Random “walkers” **carve tunnels through rock**. <br>Creates twisty labyrinths and mine-like passages. <br>Easy to tune for density or direction. |\r\n| ****Caverns Map**** | Cellular Automata | Starts from random noise, then **smooths into natural caves**. <br>Ideal for large caverns, underground lakes, or organic interiors. |\r\n\r\r\n\r\n### Tips\r\n\r\n- You can **chain generators** for hybrid maps — e.g. use *Rogue Map* to create rooms, then apply *Caverns Map* smoothing to round the interiors.  \r\n- Use [**TilemapFillRect**](action:TilemapFillRect) to quickly reset areas between tests.  \r\n- For consistent results across multiple procedural actions, initialize Random once per scene using [**RandomInitState**](action:RandomInitState).  \r\r\n\r\r\n\r\n### Next Steps\r\n\r\nThese examples form the foundation for advanced procedural systems in PlayMaker 2.  \n\r\nUpcoming samples will explore:\r\n\r\n- **Perlin / Simplex Noise** heightmaps  \r\n- **Voronoi / Region Partitioning**  \r\n- **Wave Function Collapse** for tile-based patterns  \r\n- **Hybrid Techniques** combining rooms, tunnels, and noise-based terrain\r\n\r\r\nLet us know on the forums what you'd like to see!",
